<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- The chain built from EXTERNAL events: a machine that answers an event by
     sending itself the next one, with no target, which puts it on the queue a
     host delivers to.

     W3C SCXML 3.13 ends a macrostep where nothing is enabled by NULL and the
     internal queue is empty, and Appendix D lets a macrostep fail to end. It
     says nothing of a chain of macrosteps. Every macrostep of this machine
     ends: that is why `MAX_MACROSTEP_MICROSTEPS`, the ceiling that
     `internal_chain_is_bounded` and `eventless_macrostep_is_bounded` hold, never
     applies to it. The main event loop takes the next external event whenever
     the queue is not empty, and here it never is, so a host call that drains it
     did not return.

     Measured 2026-10-02 on the Python runtime through the authoring driver,
     whose only bound on such a design was its processor-time limit: stopped
     after 25.5 s and judged the machine's doing ("another machine may differ")
     when a design that does this does it on every host. The Rust runtime's
     `run_main_event_loop` has the same shape (read, not run) and the other
     engines are held to the contract in ARCHITECTURE.md "External-Event
     Budget".

     Outcomes this document separates. Each is one machine, entered from `idle`
     and never left, so the chain of one outcome cannot answer another's:

       poke     one ordinary transition      control: proves a run fired
       spin     a chain that cannot stop     the engine takes its budget of
                                             events in the call, leaves the
                                             rest queued and says it did
       bounded  a chain that STOPS by itself exactly the budget a test gives
                                             it, and refused nothing: a long
                                             chain is not a runaway
       resume   a chain LONGER than a call   the refused events were left on
                but finite                   the queue: the next call finishes
                                             them
       zero     a chain through the clock    `delay="0ms"`: due at the instant
                that never advances          being processed. An engine reads a
                                             STATIC zero delay as undelayed (the
                                             Rust template does) or hands it to
                                             its scheduler (not measured on all
                                             of them), so what this holds is
                                             only that the call returns
       zero_expr the same, through          `delayexpr="'0ms'"`: an expression
                 an expression              has no static value to read as
                                             undelayed, so an engine that
                                             schedules it pops a due entry,
                                             runs the handler, and finds it has
                                             armed another that is due NOW. Each
                                             pass takes one event, so a budget
                                             on the drain alone never trips and
                                             the popping call never returns.
                                             This is the outcome that holds the
                                             same-instant bound (ARCHITECTURE.md
                                             rule 5) on an engine whose
                                             scheduler can do that
       timed    a chain that is finite       the events are due at LATER
                because the clock is         instants, so the budget does not
                                             refuse them however small it is

     `bounded` is exactly six events and that is deliberate. The host's own
     event counts as the first of a call (the engine takes it off the queue, or
     processes it directly where it has no queue to take it from, and either way
     it is the first), so `bounded` is the host's `bounded`, then five `lap`s:
     four that send the next and a fifth that sends nothing. A budget of six
     takes all of them and refuses nothing, a budget of five refuses the call
     with one `lap` still queued. An off-by-one in either direction is
     arithmetic here, not a judgement.

     `spin` counts the events it handles in `links`: the host's `spin` is the
     first event of the call, so a budget of B leaves `links == B - 1` and one
     `link` queued.

     Deliberately single-axis: no `<raise>`, no `<invoke>`, no `<parallel>`, no
     failing expression. `internal_chain_is_bounded` owns the chain built from
     `<raise>`, `eventless_macrostep_is_bounded` the one built from transitions
     that need no event, `error_cascade_is_bounded` the one built from errors.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0" datamodel="ecmascript" initial="idle"
       name="external_chain_is_bounded">
    <datamodel>
        <!-- Written by the control transition only. A host that reads a
             counter of zero cannot tell "the run never happened" from "the
             run happened and did nothing". -->
        <data id="pokes" expr="0"/>
        <!-- Events the unbounded chain handled. -->
        <data id="links" expr="0"/>
        <!-- Events the finite chain handled: five, the last of which sends
             nothing. -->
        <data id="laps" expr="0"/>
        <!-- Events of the chain longer than a call: thirty. -->
        <data id="beats" expr="0"/>
        <!-- Events the zero-delay chain handled. How many is NOT asserted: it
             depends on whether an engine delivers a zero delay through its
             scheduler or straight to the queue, and what is held is that the
             call returns. -->
        <data id="blinks" expr="0"/>
        <!-- The same, through `delayexpr`. -->
        <data id="exprs" expr="0"/>
        <!-- Events of the chain that is finite because the clock is. -->
        <data id="pulses" expr="0"/>
    </datamodel>

    <state id="idle">
        <transition event="poke" target="idle">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="spin" target="spin"/>
        <transition event="bounded" target="bounded"/>
        <transition event="resume" target="resuming"/>
        <transition event="zero" target="zeroing"/>
        <transition event="zero_expr" target="zeroing_expr"/>
        <transition event="timed" target="timing"/>
    </state>

    <!-- A chain that cannot end. The state is entered once, so its `onentry`
         runs once and starts the chain; every link answers by sending the next.
         The transitions are TARGETLESS: a self transition would re-run
         `onentry` and start a second chain beside the first, and the count
         would then measure two. -->
    <state id="spin">
        <onentry><send event="link"/></onentry>
        <transition event="link">
            <assign location="links" expr="links + 1"/>
            <send event="link"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>

    <!-- Five events and then nothing: four links that each send the next, and a
         fifth that is the last. Two transitions rather than an `<if>` so the
         choice is document-order selection, which every backend resolves the
         same way. -->
    <state id="bounded">
        <onentry><send event="lap"/></onentry>
        <transition event="lap" cond="laps &lt; 4">
            <assign location="laps" expr="laps + 1"/>
            <send event="lap"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="lap">
            <assign location="laps" expr="laps + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>

    <!-- Thirty events: longer than any call a test gives it a budget for, and
         finite. What it measures is what the refusal did with the events it
         would not take: an engine that dropped the queue stops short and never
         finishes, and one that ran the chain anyway finishes it in the first
         call. Neither can be told from the correct answer by any other outcome
         here. -->
    <state id="resuming">
        <onentry><send event="beat"/></onentry>
        <transition event="beat" cond="beats &lt; 29">
            <assign location="beats" expr="beats + 1"/>
            <send event="beat"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="beat">
            <assign location="beats" expr="beats + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>

    <!-- A chain through a delay of zero. It is due at the instant being
         processed, so an engine whose scheduler delivers it as a due entry pops
         it, runs the handler, and finds the handler has armed another that is
         due now. Each pass takes one event off the queue, so a budget on the
         drain alone never trips, and the call that is popping never returns.
         The clock never moves. -->
    <state id="zeroing">
        <onentry><send event="blink" delay="0ms"/></onentry>
        <transition event="blink">
            <assign location="blinks" expr="blinks + 1"/>
            <send event="blink" delay="0ms"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>

    <!-- The same chain through an expression. A delay that is an expression has
         no value until it runs, so there is nothing to read at generation time
         and an engine cannot take it for undelayed: whatever it does with a
         delay that evaluates to zero is what this measures. The document is an
         ecmascript one for that reason; `sce-static` refuses `delayexpr`. -->
    <state id="zeroing_expr">
        <onentry><send event="blink" delayexpr="'0ms'"/></onentry>
        <transition event="blink">
            <assign location="exprs" expr="exprs + 1"/>
            <send event="blink" delayexpr="'0ms'"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>

    <!-- Eight events, each due one millisecond after the last, armed together
         when the state is entered. Walked a millisecond at a time they come one
         to a call; moved by one jump of eight they come eight in one call. Either
         way every one is due at a LATER instant than the one before, so the
         chain is finite because the clock is, and it is not a runaway however
         small the budget: a legitimate time-driven workload is not refused. -->
    <state id="timing">
        <onentry>
            <send event="pulse" delay="1ms"/>
            <send event="pulse" delay="2ms"/>
            <send event="pulse" delay="3ms"/>
            <send event="pulse" delay="4ms"/>
            <send event="pulse" delay="5ms"/>
            <send event="pulse" delay="6ms"/>
            <send event="pulse" delay="7ms"/>
            <send event="pulse" delay="8ms"/>
        </onentry>
        <transition event="pulse">
            <assign location="pulses" expr="pulses + 1"/>
        </transition>
        <transition event="poke">
            <assign location="pokes" expr="pokes + 1"/>
        </transition>
    </state>
</scxml>
